    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 211035\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n335 AM PDT Mon Sep 21 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n* Showers and Thunderstorms expected over Inyo, Esmeralda, Nye, and \n  Lincoln County later today, persisting into tonight.\n\n* Near normal temperatures early this week, warming back up to above \n  normal mid week into next weekend. \n\n* Potential increase in monsoonal moisture for Mohave County late \n  week\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...through Monday.\n\nSatellite imagery shows an upper low off the coast of Central \nCalifornia, bringing high clouds to the region during the early \nhours Monday morning. This system will slowly track east into \nCentral Nevada over the next 48 hours bringing a chance for showers \nand thunderstorms over the Sierra and portions of the Great Basin \nthis afternoon through Tuesday. Moisture advection ahead of the \napproaching trough is only marginal with PW ranging from 0.7 to 1.00 \ninch surging northward through the Great Basin; however forecast \nsoundings for Desert Rock, Tonopah, and Bishop show fairly deep \nsaturation through the atmospheric profile to support convective \nactivity. CAPE ranges from around 300-600 j/kg with higher DCAPE \nvalues while 0-6km shear is more than enough for convection to \ndevelop and sustain itself after sunset. Best forcing for ascent \nlooks to be over the Sierra and in higher terrain within Esmeralda, \nNye, and Lincoln Counties. Both 00z HRRR and RRFS are in great \nagreement of convection developing in these areas after 19z, then \npersisting through 06-08z at least. While moisture availability and \ninstability are only marginal, if storms end up training over the \nsame areas then flooding could become a concern around Death Valley \nand into the Great Basin. DCAPE is high enough where gusty winds \nwill be possible in any storm along with heavy rain and lightning. \nFurther south toward Las Vegas, the I-15 corridor and points south, \nnot expecting much, if any, convection as the main forcing for \nascent remains to the north. Will have to watch if any outflow \nboundaries track south toward the Las Vegas Valley Monday evening as \nsome hi-res guidance shifts winds around to the north. Confidence is \nlow if Las Vegas will see gusty winds from storm outflow, but will \nmonitor storm trends and evolution through the evening. \n\nWith the upper low passing overhead bringing slightly cooler air \naloft, temperatures will cool marginally behind the low on Tuesday \nwith most locations 3 to 6 degrees cooler than today. The low will \neject fairly quickly to the northeast on Tuesday morning but there \nmay be a few lingering showers into the early afternoon in Lincoln \nCounty. \n\nGenerally, dry southwesterly flow and subtle ridging sets up for the \nremainder of the week and into next week. Medium to long range \nguidance is in agreement that low level temperatures will run \nseveral degrees above normal in this upcoming pattern, resulting in \na warming trend each day mid week through the weekend. High \ntemperatures by Saturday reach the low 100s again in desert valleys. \nOverall Heat Risk through the week remains low, with sporadic areas \nof moderate. Along with warming temps, ensemble guidance is also \nhinting towards a modest uptick in moisture mainly south and east of \nLas Vegas on Thursday and Friday. Currently, the best chances of \nprecipitation associated with this moisture are around 10 to 20 \npercent in Mohave County. \n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 12Z Forecast Package...Winds\nwill follow typical daily directional patterns with speeds under \n10 knots through late morning when breezy southeasterly winds \nwill pick up. These southeasterly winds will be accompanied by 15 \nto 20 knot wind gusts and will continue into the early evening \nhours when gusts will drop off and winds will become more \nsoutherly. Southerly winds will continue late into the evening \nwhen gusty northwesterly winds will pick up as a front moves \nthrough the valley. Mid and high cloud cover will increase \nthroughout the day, with bases at or above 12kft. Showers are \npossible over the mountains surrounding the Las Vegas Valley \nduring the afternoon and evening, but these are not expected to \nreach the terminal.\n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast\nCalifornia...For the 12Z Forecast Package...Outside of the \nwestern Mojave Desert, including KDAG, where 8 to 12 knot westerly\nwinds will continue into the early evening hours, winds across \nthe rest of the region will be light and variable into the late \nmorning hours. These light and variable winds will give way to \nbreezy southerly-to- southeasterly winds accompanied by 15 to 25 \nknot wind gusts beginning later this morning/early this afternoon.\nGusty westerly winds will pick up across the western Mojave \nDesert, including KDAG, after sunset while the rest of the area \nsees winds relax during the early evening hours. Cloud cover will \nincrease through the day with bases at or above 10kft. Showers and\nthunderstorms develop across the southern Great Basin and the \nhigher terrain of Inyo County, some of which may reach KBIH. \nErratic winds, moderate-to-heavy rain, decreased visibility, and \nlower CIGS will be possible in and around any storms that develop.\nA front will work its way through the region overnight, causing\nwinds to pick up and shift to the north as it passes.\n\n&&\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...Moore\nAVIATION...Stessman\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
